From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0245] clock: pulse the NPU reset, once, on a block that is stopped

Every card so far read, gated, shared or voted. None of them drove a reset
line, and a reset is the one write whose effect is invisible from here: the
vendor's drivers/clk/qcom/reset.c does nothing but set a bit, wait, and clear
it again - qcom_reset_set_assert() is a regmap_update_bits() with the same mask
in both directions, and the wait is udelay(map->udelay ?: 1). A pulse on the
wrong block is therefore indistinguishable from a pulse on the right one
unless the ring says what the block was doing before and after.

Which block. GCC_NPU_BCR is reset ID 3 of the vendor binding, at vendor offset
0x4d000 - word 0 of the page C2-06 already maps, so no new mapping and no
seventh window. Bit 0 is BLK_ARES with the polarity the hardware header gives
it: 1 asserts, 0 releases. The block is the sample because it is stopped and
has no owner: 0137 read its three voted branches as 0x80000000 and 0138 put a
ballot in and took it back out, and C0-02 finds no consumer for the NPU
anywhere in the tree. The other BCRs the page offers were all rejected for a
reason worth writing down - PDM shares its page with the gcc_pdm2_clk_src RCG
C2-02 measures, and MMSS/GPU/VIDEO would reset subsystems that are running.

Six steps, one claim per row. The pass reads the four CBCRs of the page, then
the BCR word; a block that is claimed (bit 0 already set) or running (halt
status clear) is refused with zero stores, and the value stays in the row as
the evidence. With the block idle it stores assert | (arrival & config mask),
reads the word back, and waits the vendor's 1 us - the readback comes before
the delay so a store that never landed costs no time. The release stores
(arrival & config mask) & ~bit and reads back again; the comparison is made
inside the config mask because a BCR may report status bits its FSM owns, and
calling that a failed restore would be wrong in the loud direction.

The arrival state wins. A block that arrives held in reset is released by the
pulse - that is what a reset is - but leaving it released would be the one
durable side effect this card must not have, so a third store puts the arrival
word back and the row says restored=1. A block that fails to latch the assert
is restored the same way, gets detail=assert-not-landed and NotReady, and
prints no after= or delay-us=: no pulse happened, so there is nothing to
timing-account.

The verdict, and its limit. asserted= and released= are readbacks of bit 0,
before=/after= are the whole word, delay-us= is the pulse width, and
collateral= is whether the four CBCRs of the page were unchanged; the summary
is in the start row as reads= and writes= like every earlier card. What it is
not: a functional claim. The NPU has no driver and its GDSC is off, so the
only witness that the block reacted is its own status bit, and the card says
that instead of dressing it up.

Findings are recorded, not washed. A neighbour that moved is
detail=collateral-moved with the count and NotReady, a release that did not
land keeps the release readback under detail=release-not-landed and NotReady,
and a restore that mismatched is named before the word is put back. Every one
of those paths still holds to the same internal accounting: stores= counts the
stores the pass issued, writes= the register writes the backend logged.

Identity without a descriptor. The reset has no entry in gccClocks[] - a
descriptor would add a row to images that never map this page - so it carries
its own local ID 3 and its own node name, and its store path is
writeResetRegister(), bounded to the one word it owns so the generic four-window
bound stays intact. The fifth window is mapped by either card, and the map row
names which one: an image that arms the reset prints reset=1, not vote-probe=1.
The probe and the reset both own that page and there is one row to explain it,
so the two properties are refused together at start, as a vote and a reset
already were.

Backward compatibility is the rule the earlier cards set: without
sweet,clock-reset the image maps four windows, prints the 0137 start row
unchanged and performs zero stores. The ADT generator emits the property and
the fifth range as one step, implies the GCC node, and the tree it writes is
removable back to the plain GCC bytes - that is asserted, not asserted-by-eye.

Host tests cover the accepted pass (two ordered stores, one 1 us delay, 30
reads and 2 writes, the arrival word back), a block that arrived held (three
stores, held-arrival=1, restored=1), a claimed and a running block (refused,
zero stores), a block that never latched the assert (restored, no timing),
a neighbour that moved inside the page, the two bad-property values, and the
two arm-time refusals (vote beside a reset, probe beside a reset).

